; spell/src/spell/app.bl — the manifest: what spell IS, as one require head.
;
; Loading an application means loading its namespaces in dependency order.
; That order is already stated, precisely, in each file's `(:require …)` — so
; the only thing missing was a single ROOT to pull on. This file is that root
; and nothing else: it defines no vars, because a manifest that also held code
; would invite the code to grow and the manifest to rot.
;
; ── why this exists rather than a list in Elixir ────────────────────────────
;
; `BeamLisp.Spell.load!/1` previously carried the list:
;
;   ~w(spell.seam spell.contract spell.machine spell.provider spell.seed)
;
; which is the application's composition written in the host language, one
; import away from the code it describes. Every wave that adds a namespace has
; to remember to edit it, and forgetting produces the quietest possible failure:
; the module simply is not there, and the first symptom is an "undefined var"
; from somewhere unrelated.
;
; A glob (`spell/src/spell/*.bl`) was the other candidate. It is worse in two
; specific ways: it loads whatever happens to be on disk — a scratch file, a
; half-finished module — and it decides load ORDER by filename, which is not
; the dependency order and only coincidentally works.
;
; ── what belongs here ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The namespaces that make up the application. NOT the language libraries they
; require (`rewrite` lives in priv/ and is pulled in by whoever needs it), and
; NOT test namespaces.

(ns spell.app
  (:require [spell.seam]
            [spell.contract]
            [spell.machine]
            [spell.provider]
            [spell.seed]))
